<description>
Merge same-sized filled rectangular objects by overlaying their internal marker pixels.

Input structure:
- A dominant background surrounds two or more separated 4-connected foreground objects.
- Every object is a filled rectangle of the same size.
- Each rectangle has the same majority base color plus one or more non-base marker pixels.

Rule:
- Create one output rectangle with the shared object size, filled with the shared base color.
- For each input rectangle, copy every non-base marker pixel to the same relative location
  in the output.
- Marker overlays must either land on base color or agree in color with an existing marker.

Key insight:
The separated rectangles are layers of the same template; their colored marker pixels
combine by local coordinates while the repeated base fill is just the output canvas.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "non-base marker pixel s inside a same-sized filled rectangle",
  "dst": "cell d in the shared output rectangle",
  "match_cond": "s.local_position == d.local_position",
  "transform": "fill the output with the shared base color; paint each matched d with s.color",
  "grid_shape": "one shared object bbox"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task: Merge same-sized filled rectangles by overlaying their marker pixels at relative positions.
  grid: 14-18 rows x 14-18 cols (square), roughly 3x the rectangle side length
  background: single color filling all space outside rectangles
  rectangles:
    count: 2-4
    shape: identical HxW across all rectangles, H and W in 4-6, H==W [observed]
    base_color: single non-bg color shared by all rectangles; majority of each rectangle
    placement: scattered, non-overlapping, separated by >=1 bg cell on each axis; >=1 bg padding on all grid edges
  markers:
    per_object: 1 connected component of 1-4 pixels per rectangle, single unique color per rectangle
    color: each rectangle has a distinct marker color; all marker colors differ from bg and base
    position: strictly interior (never on rectangle border row/col) [observed]
    no_overlap: marker regions from different rectangles must occupy disjoint relative positions within the HxW template
    coverage: combined markers fill 25-32% of HxW area [observed]
  output: HxW rectangle filled with base_color, all marker pixels overlaid at their relative positions

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick H=W from 4-6. Choose bg, base, and N (2-4) distinct marker colors (all different).
    2. Build the HxW output canvas filled with base_color.
    3. For each of N objects, place a connected 1-4 pixel marker blob of its unique color
       in the interior (rows 1..H-2, cols 1..W-2), ensuring no overlap with existing markers.
       Use flood-fill or L-shaped templates. Verify combined coverage is reasonable.
    4. Decompose the output into N layers: each layer is HxW filled with base_color,
       with only its own marker pixels retained.
    5. Choose grid size ~3x the rectangle side. Place N rectangles (the layers) on the grid
       with >=1 bg gap between any pair and >=1 bg border padding. Fill remaining cells with bg.
```
